AHS-011 — Control Flow

Document ID: AHS-011
Title: Control Flow
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

Architectural Views: Process

1. Purpose

This specification defines the canonical control flow of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

Control flow governs the execution of engineering cognition by determining the sequencing, branching, synchronization, coordination, and completion of cognitive activities while preserving the canonical semantics defined by ACC and AHS.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Execution sequencing
Branching
Synchronization
Parallel execution
State transitions
Error handling
Human intervention
Completion criteria

It does not define workflow engines or execution technologies.

3. Canonical Definition

Control Flow is the ordered coordination of cognitive activities that governs how engineering information progresses through the Atlas Cognitive Pipeline while preserving correctness, consistency, and traceability.

4. Control Flow Principles

Atlas control flow SHALL:

Preserve the logical cognitive pipeline.
Ensure deterministic execution where required.
Support asynchronous execution.
Support parallel execution where semantically valid.
Prevent invalid execution sequences.
Preserve auditability.
Preserve recoverability.
5. Canonical Execution Model
Start
   │
   ▼
Observe
   │
   ▼
Validate Evidence
   │
   ▼
Build Understanding
   │
   ▼
Persist Memory
   │
   ▼
Update Knowledge
   │
   ▼
Reason
   │
   ▼
Execute Capability
   │
   ▼
Generate Decision Support
   │
   ▼
End

This logical sequence SHALL be preserved regardless of runtime execution strategy.

6. Control Decisions

Control flow MAY evaluate conditions at defined decision points.

Typical decisions include:

Is sufficient evidence available?
Is confidence acceptable?
Are constraints satisfied?
Is human approval required?
Can reasoning proceed?
Should recommendations be generated?

Every decision SHALL be recorded as part of the execution trace.

7. Branching

Atlas supports conditional branching.

Example:

Evidence
    │
    ▼
Confidence Check
      │
 ┌────┴────┐
 │         │
 ▼         ▼
Continue  Acquire More Evidence

Branch conditions SHALL be deterministic and reproducible.

8. Parallel Execution

Independent cognitive activities MAY execute concurrently.

Examples include:

Evidence
    │
 ┌──┴──────────┐
 ▼             ▼
Memory     Knowledge Update

Parallel execution SHALL:

Preserve data consistency.
Preserve ordering constraints.
Synchronize before dependent stages execute.
9. Synchronization

Synchronization points SHALL ensure that required cognitive artifacts are available before dependent execution continues.

Example:

Understanding
      │
 ┌────┴────┐
 ▼         ▼
Memory   Knowledge
 └────┬────┘
      ▼
Reasoning

Reasoning SHALL NOT execute until all mandatory inputs are available.

10. Human Control Points

Atlas SHALL support explicit human interaction during workflow execution.

Typical control points include:

Evidence review
Design approval
Constraint override
Safety validation
Final recommendation approval

Human actions SHALL become part of the execution history.

11. Exception Handling

When execution cannot proceed:

Atlas SHALL:

Halt the affected activity.
Preserve intermediate artifacts.
Record failure details.
Notify dependent services.
Support retry or recovery.
Escalate when necessary.

Invalid outputs SHALL NOT continue downstream.

12. Completion Criteria

A workflow SHALL complete only when:

Required stages have executed.
Mandatory artifacts exist.
Constraints are satisfied.
Required approvals are complete.
Decision Support artifacts have been generated or the workflow has been explicitly terminated.
13. Control Flow Metadata

Every workflow SHALL maintain execution metadata including:

Workflow ID
Current state
Active stage
Previous stage
Next stage
Decision history
Retry count
Human interactions
Start time
End time
Completion status
14. Conformance

An implementation conforms if it:

Preserves the canonical execution sequence.
Implements deterministic control decisions.
Supports branching and synchronization.
Records execution history.
Maintains recoverability and traceability.
15. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Adaptive execution policies
Distributed control coordination
Policy-driven branching
Autonomous workflow optimization
Multi-agent execution control

These enhancements SHALL preserve the canonical cognitive semantics.